(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_physics_dualnatureofradiationandmatter=[{id:"neet-2026-q002",pn:2,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter",st:"Wave and particle nature of light",q:`Match List I with List II :
List I \u2014 List II
A. E = h\u03BD \u2014 I. de Broglie wavelength
B. Diffraction and Interference \u2014 II. Particle nature of light
C. \u03BB = h/p \u2014 III. Wave nature of light
D. Compton effect \u2014 IV. Energy of photon
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I"],c:0,s:`A. E = h\u03BD is the energy of a photon.
B. Diffraction and interference show the wave nature of light.
C. \u03BB = h/p is the de Broglie wavelength of a particle.
D. The Compton effect shows the particle nature of light.
So the match is A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q014",pn:14,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter",st:"Photoelectric effect graphs",q:"A beam of light falls on a metal surface such that photo-electrons are generated. If power of the light source starts to decrease linearly with time t, then variation of the photocurrent I and magnitude of the stopping potential |V| with time is best represented by:",o:["I decreases linearly with t (straight line falling to zero); |V| decreases linearly with t","I is constant with t; |V| decreases linearly with t","I is constant with t; |V| is constant with t","I decreases linearly with t; |V| is constant with t"],c:3,s:`Power of the source decreases, so the number of photons per second decreases linearly with time. The source is the same, so the energy of each photon is the same.
Power P \u221D nE, so P \u221D n (number of photons per second).
So photocurrent I decreases linearly with time.
Photon energy is constant, so the stopping potential |V| stays constant with time.`,fig:{w:440,h:195,alt:"Four options, each a pair of graphs of photocurrent I and stopping potential |V| against time t: (1) both fall linearly, (2) I constant and |V| falls linearly, (3) both constant, (4) I falls linearly and |V| is constant.",d:[["t",2,44,"(1)"],["l",42,70,42,8,"a"],["l",42,70,130,70,"a"],["l",42,20,97,70,""],["t",36,25,"I","e s"],["t",82,83,"t","m s"],["l",142,70,142,8,"a"],["l",142,70,230,70,"a"],["l",142,20,197,70,""],["t",136,25,"|V|","e s"],["t",182,83,"t","m s"],["t",212,44,"(2)"],["l",252,70,252,8,"a"],["l",252,70,340,70,"a"],["l",252,20,307,20,""],["t",246,25,"I","e s"],["t",292,83,"t","m s"],["l",352,70,352,8,"a"],["l",352,70,440,70,"a"],["l",352,20,407,70,""],["t",346,25,"|V|","e s"],["t",392,83,"t","m s"],["t",2,144,"(3)"],["l",42,170,42,108,"a"],["l",42,170,130,170,"a"],["l",42,120,97,120,""],["t",36,125,"I","e s"],["t",82,183,"t","m s"],["l",142,170,142,108,"a"],["l",142,170,230,170,"a"],["l",142,120,197,120,""],["t",136,125,"|V|","e s"],["t",182,183,"t","m s"],["t",212,144,"(4)"],["l",252,170,252,108,"a"],["l",252,170,340,170,"a"],["l",252,120,307,170,""],["t",246,125,"I","e s"],["t",292,183,"t","m s"],["l",352,170,352,108,"a"],["l",352,170,440,170,"a"],["l",352,120,407,120,""],["t",346,125,"|V|","e s"],["t",392,183,"t","m s"]]},sfig:{w:260,h:195,alt:"I versus t is a straight line falling to zero; |V| versus t is a horizontal line.",d:[["l",30,80,30,15,"a"],["l",30,80,105,80,"a"],["l",30,28,80,80],["t",24,24,"I","e s"],["t",65,95,"t","m s"],["l",30,165,30,100,"a"],["l",30,165,105,165,"a"],["l",30,112,80,112],["t",24,108,"|V|","e s"],["t",65,180,"t","m s"]]}},{id:"neet-2026r-q023",pn:23,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter",st:"Photoelectric cells stopping potential",q:"A ray of light with wavelength \u03BB is incident on three different photoelectric cells namely 1, 2 and 3. The threshold wavelength of these photoelectric cells are \u03BB\u2081, \u03BB\u2082, and \u03BB\u2083, respectively and the magnitude of stopping potentials of these cells are V\u2081, V\u2082 and V\u2083, respectively. The relation between \u03BB and threshold wavelengths are \u03BB\u2081 < \u03BB, \u03BB\u2082 > \u03BB and \u03BB\u2083 >> \u03BB. The correct option is:",o:["V\u2081 = 0, V\u2082 > V\u2083","V\u2081 > V\u2082, V\u2083 = 0","V\u2081 < V\u2082, V\u2083 = 0","V\u2081 = 0, V\u2082 < V\u2083"],c:3,s:`An electron is ejected only if the photon energy is greater than the work function, \u03C6 = hc/\u03BB.
For cell 1, \u03BB\u2081 < \u03BB, so \u03C6\u2081 > \u03C6 (work function of cell 1 is greater than the photon energy).
So no electron is ejected and V\u2081 = 0.
Einstein equation: photon energy \u03C6 = \u03C6\u2080 + eV.
For cells 2 and 3: \u03BB\u2082 > \u03BB and \u03BB\u2083 >> \u03BB, so \u03C6 > \u03C6\u2082 and \u03C6 >> \u03C6\u2083 (work functions are smaller than photon energy).
So V\u2083 > V\u2082.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q040",pn:40,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter",st:"Momentum of photon and electron",q:`A photon and an electron, each of 20 eV energy, move in free space. The ratio of linear momentum of electron p\u2091 to that of photon pPh, p\u2091/pPh is:
[Take speed of light = 3 \xD7 10\u2078 ms\u207B\xB9, charge of electron = \u20131.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 C and mass of electron = 9 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9 kg]`,o:["1/250","225","275","2/450"],c:1,s:`E\u2091 = EPh = 20 eV.
For the electron p\u2091 = \u221A(2m\u2091E\u2091), for the photon pPh = EPh/c.
p\u2091/pPh = (\u221A(2m\u2091E\u2091)/EPh)\xB7c = \u221A(2m\u2091/EPh)\xB7c.
p\u2091/pPh = \u221A((2 \xD7 9 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9)/(20 \xD7 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079)) \xD7 3 \xD7 10\u2078.
= (3/4) \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \xD7 3 \xD7 10\u2078.
= 900/4 = 225.`},{id:"neet-2026-q042",pn:42,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter",st:"Photoelectric effect",q:`For a metal of work function 6.6 eV, which of the following wavelengths of incident radiation does not give rise to the photoelectric effect?
(Take Planck's constant as 6.6\xD710\u207B\xB3\u2074 J s)`,o:["200 nm","150 nm","100 nm","50 nm"],c:0,s:`The photoelectric effect does not occur when hc/\u03BB < work function.
That means \u03BB > hc/W\u2080.
\u03BB > (6.6\xD710\u207B\xB3\u2074 \xD7 3\xD710\u2078)/(6.6 \xD7 1.6\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079).
\u03BB > (3\xD710\u207B\u2077)/1.6 m = 300/1.6 nm.
\u03BB > 187.5 nm.
So 200 nm is the wavelength with no photoelectric effect.`},{id:"neet-2025-q037",pn:37,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter",st:"de Broglie wavelength",q:"A photon and an electron (mass m) have the same energy E. The ratio (\u03BB_photon/\u03BB_electron) of their de Broglie wavelengths is: (c is the speed of light)",o:["\u221A(E/2m)","c\u221A(2mE)","c\u221A(2m/E)","(1/c)\u221A(E/2m)"],c:2,s:`For the photon, E = hc/\u03BB_Ph, so \u03BB_Ph = hc/E.
For the electron, p is the momentum and E = p\xB2/2m = (h/\u03BB_e)\xB2 \xD7 1/(2m).
So \u03BB_e = h/\u221A(2mE).
\u03BB_Ph/\u03BB_e = (hc/E)/(h/\u221A(2mE)) = c\u221A(2m/E).`},{id:"neet-2025-q038",pn:38,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter",st:"Photoelectric effect graphs",q:`Which of the following options represent the variation of photoelectric current with property of light shown on the x-axis?
A. Graph of photoelectric current (y-axis) against Intensity of light (x-axis): a straight line rising from the origin.
B. Photoelectric current against Intensity of light: a horizontal straight line (constant current).
C. Photoelectric current against Frequency of light: a straight line rising from the origin.
D. Photoelectric current against Frequency of light: a straight line starting on the x-axis at a positive frequency (not at the origin) and rising.`,o:["A only","A and C","A and D","B and D"],c:0,s:`Photoelectric current is directly proportional to the intensity of light.
So the graph of current against intensity is a straight line through the origin (graph A).`,fig:{w:489.8,h:272,alt:"Four graphs of photoelectric current: A against intensity, a straight line from the origin; B against intensity, a horizontal line; C against frequency, a straight line from the origin; D against frequency, a line starting on the axis at a positive frequency",d:[["l",62,112.3,62,28.6,"a"],["l",62,112.3,173.6,112.3,"a"],["t",53.3,118.5,"0","s"],["t",69.4,130,"Intensity of light","s"],["t",65.7,23.6,"Photoelectric current","s"],["l",62,112.3,143.8,36,"B"],["t",5,79.4,"A."],["l",372.6,112.3,372.6,28.6,"a"],["l",372.6,112.3,483.6,112.3,"a"],["t",363.9,118.5,"0","s"],["t",380.1,130,"Intensity of light","s"],["t",376.3,23.6,"Photoelectric current","s"],["l",372.6,49.7,476.8,49.7,"B"],["t",315.6,79.4,"B."],["l",62,234.8,62,153.6,"a"],["l",62,234.8,173.6,234.8,"a"],["t",53.3,241,"0","s"],["t",69.4,262.7,"Frequency of light","s"],["t",65.7,148.6,"Photoelectric current","s"],["l",62,234.8,156.9,160.4,"B"],["t",5,203.8,"C."],["l",372.6,234.8,372.6,153.6,"a"],["l",372.6,234.8,483.6,234.8,"a"],["t",363.9,241,"0","s"],["t",380.1,262.7,"Frequency of light","s"],["t",376.3,148.6,"Photoelectric current","s"],["l",403,234.8,484.2,162.9,"B"],["t",315.6,203.8,"D."]]},sfig:{w:220,h:190,alt:"Graph of photoelectric current against intensity of light: straight line through the origin",d:[["l",23,147,23,15,"a"],["l",23,147,202,147,"a"],["l",23,147,155,26,"B"],["t",35,175,"Intensity of light","s"],["t",32,10,"Photoelectric current","s"]]}},{id:"neet-2024-q011",pn:11,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter",st:"de Broglie wavelength and kinetic energy",q:"The graph which shows the variation of (1/\u03BB\xB2) and its kinetic energy, E is (where \u03BB is de Broglie wavelength of a free particle)",o:["graph 1","graph 2","graph 3","graph 4"],c:1,s:`de Broglie wavelength \u03BB = h/p = h/(mv) = h/\u221A(2mE), where E = \xBDmv\xB2.
Squaring both sides: \u03BB\xB2 = h\xB2/(2mE).
So 1/\u03BB\xB2 = (2m/h\xB2) E, which is a constant times E.
The graph is a straight line through the origin with constant slope.
Note: The printed solution has \u03BB\xB2 = h\xB2/(4m\xB2E), a misprint; the correct form is h\xB2/(2mE). The conclusion is unchanged.`,fig:{w:320,h:230,alt:"Four graphs of 1/\u03BB\xB2 against E: (1) rising concave-down curve from the origin, (2) straight line through the origin, (3) rising concave-up curve, (4) falling curve.",d:[["l",34,92,34,12,"a"],["l",34,92,136,92,"a"],["t",32,40,"1/\u03BB\xB2","e"],["t",28,105,"O","e"],["t",140,96,"E"],["t",2,80,"(1)"],["p","M34 92 C36 55 65 33 100 30",""],["l",199,92,199,12,"a"],["l",199,92,301,92,"a"],["t",197,40,"1/\u03BB\xB2","e"],["t",193,105,"O","e"],["t",305,96,"E"],["t",167,80,"(2)"],["p","M199 92 L270 30",""],["l",34,207,34,127,"a"],["l",34,207,136,207,"a"],["t",32,155,"1/\u03BB\xB2","e"],["t",28,220,"O","e"],["t",140,211,"E"],["t",2,195,"(3)"],["p","M34 207 C70 205 98 187 102 143",""],["l",199,207,199,127,"a"],["l",199,207,301,207,"a"],["t",197,155,"1/\u03BB\xB2","e"],["t",193,220,"O","e"],["t",305,211,"E"],["t",167,195,"(4)"],["p","M205 143 C207 185 235 203 290 205",""]]}},{id:"neet-2024-q020",pn:20,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter",st:"Photon properties",q:`If c is the velocity of light in free space, the correct statements about photon among the following are
A. The energy of a photon is E = h\u03BD.
B. The velocity of a photon is c.
C. The momentum of a photon, p = h\u03BD/c.
D. In a photon-electron collision, both total energy and total momentum are conserved.
E. Photon possesses positive charge.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["A, C and D only","A, B, D and E only","A and B only","A, B, C and D only"],c:3,s:`A: the energy of a photon is E = h\u03BD.
B: a photon moves with the speed of light, c.
C: \u03BB = h/p, so p = h/\u03BB = h\u03BD/c.
D: in a photon-electron collision both total energy and total momentum are conserved.
E is false: a photon has no charge.
So A, B, C and D are correct.`}]})();
